// ======================================================================
// Build stamp. Shown on the sign-in screen and in the footer so you can
// tell at a glance whether the deployed site is the version you pushed —
// GitHub Pages and the browser cache can both lag a commit behind.
//
// `build` matches the ?v= cache-busting number on every asset URL in
// index.html; bump both together when releasing.
// ======================================================================
const APP_BUILD = {
  version: "1.0",
  // Two number series, one per channel. Production builds are plain integers
  // (…, 226, 227) on main. Beta-channel builds are five digits, NNNII — NNN
  // the cycle, II the iteration — so 22701 renders as v1.0.227-beta.1 and can
  // never collide with a production number. On release: merge beta into main,
  // set build to a production integer, and consolidate the cycle's changelog
  // entries into one entry. The What's-new overlay compares numerically per
  // origin, and both series are monotone on their own origin.
  //
  // `cycle` IS NOT DERIVED FROM `build`, and that is the whole point. It used
  // to be — Math.floor(build / 100) — which silently rolled over the moment a
  // cycle passed its 99th iteration: 25100 rendered as v1.0.251-beta.0, so
  // from build 25100 to 25112 the sign-in screen announced a beta of v1.0.251,
  // a production build that had already shipped 30 releases earlier. Stating
  // the cycle outright makes the iteration count unbounded (beta.112, beta.250,
  // as far as a cycle runs) and the rollover impossible. Set it once per cycle,
  // when the first beta build after a production release is cut.
  //
  // A note on what `cycle` means now: it was originally read as "the production
  // build this cycle will become", which stopped being true once items began
  // graduating to production one queue number at a time — production walked
  // 250 → 281 while this cycle stayed 250. It is a cycle NAME, not a promise.
  cycle: 250,
  build: 25254,
  date: "2026-09-02",
  // When this build was cut, UTC — set it with `date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%MZ`,
  // never by hand. Builds 25090-25092 and 277 carried a local Amsterdam time
  // in this field, so the sign-in stamp read over an hour into the future:
  // typing a local time into a field labelled UTC is the same mistake the
  // local-time display exists to stop somebody making.
  // Shown on the sign-in screen with the version:
  // the date alone cannot tell two releases of the same day apart, and "is the
  // thing I just pushed actually live?" is a question about minutes, not days.
  released: "2026-09-03T06:46Z",
  get isBeta() { return this.build >= 10000; },
  // Stored UTC, shown in the reader's own timezone with the offset named.
  // A build is cut once, so one absolute instant is the right thing to record —
  // but "when was this last updated?" is asked by somebody sitting in a
  // timezone, and an ISO Z stamp makes every one of them do the arithmetic.
  // The UTC original stays reachable in the element's tooltip.
  get stamp() { return `${this.label} · ${this.releasedLocal}`; },
  get releasedLocal() {
    const raw = String(this.released || "");
    const d = new Date(raw);
    if (isNaN(d.getTime())) return raw.replace("T", " ");    // unparseable — show it raw rather than "Invalid Date"
    try {
      const parts = new Intl.DateTimeFormat(undefined, {
        year: "numeric", month: "2-digit", day: "2-digit",
        hour: "2-digit", minute: "2-digit", hourCycle: "h23", timeZoneName: "short",
      }).formatToParts(d);
      const g = (t) => (parts.find((x) => x.type === t) || {}).value || "";
      if (!g("year")) return raw.replace("T", " ");
      const zone = g("timeZoneName");
      return `${g("year")}-${g("month")}-${g("day")} ${g("hour")}:${g("minute")}${zone ? ` ${zone}` : ""}`;
    } catch { return raw.replace("T", " "); }                 // Intl is optional in principle
  },
  get releasedUtc() { return String(this.released || "").replace("T", " ").replace("Z", " UTC"); },
  get timeZone() { try { return Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone || ""; } catch { return ""; } },
  // The iteration is whatever is left above the cycle's base, so it keeps
  // counting past 99 instead of wrapping into the next hundred. `cycle` is
  // missing on nothing that ships, but fall back to the old division rather
  // than print NaN if it ever is.
  get iteration() {
    return this.cycle ? this.build - this.cycle * 100 : this.build % 100;
  },
  get label() {
    return this.isBeta
      ? `v${this.version}.${this.cycle || Math.floor(this.build / 100)}-beta.${this.iteration}`
      : `v${this.version}.${this.build}`;
  },
  get full() { return `${this.label} · ${this.date}`; },
};

// Per-tool versions, keyed by the tile id in index.html. Each tool moves at
// its own pace, so a tenant-side reviewer can tell which one changed since
// they last looked without diffing the whole app. Bump the tool you touched.
//
// A tool that has reached PRODUCTION is at least 1.0. Whatever it accumulated
// on the beta channel, something a customer can open is not a 0.x thing — and
// it is the BETA chip, not the version number, that says "still proving
// itself". Only beta-channel-only tools stay below 1.0.
//
// ── `t` — THE TOOL'S PERMANENT NUMBER (roadmap R33) ────────────────────
//
// The roadmap items carry Rnn references so one can be pointed at without
// quoting its title. The tools went by name and emoji, which drift: tools get
// renamed, retitled and — once R26 lands — translated, so "the group tool" is
// already ambiguous and a Dutch interface would make every English note about
// a tool unfindable. `t` is the same discipline for the tools themselves.
//
// THE RULES, and they are the whole value of the number:
//
//   * Assigned in the ORDER THE TOOL ENTERED ENCA. T01–T31 were reconstructed
//     from git — the first commit that put each tile in index.html, ordered by
//     commit time, with tools that arrived in one commit ordered as they were
//     written into the page. That is a fact about the repository, not a
//     judgement, so it can be checked rather than argued about.
//   * NEVER REUSED. Not when a tool is retired, not when it is folded into
//     another. A recycled number makes every older note about it wrong, which
//     is precisely the failure the number exists to prevent.
//   * A NEW TOOL TAKES THE NEXT FREE NUMBER — 36 next — and takes it in the
//     same commit that adds the tile, like the changelog entry and the
//     promotion-queue row.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT NUMBERED: ❓ Help, 📋 What's new and 🗺 Roadmap.
// They are the app describing itself rather than tools that read a tenant,
// produce a report about it or write to it — which is also why they carry no
// version. They go by name, and that is stated rather than left as a gap
// somebody later "fixes" by giving them numbers and shifting everything.
//
// The entries below are ordered by where a tool sits on the home page, not by
// its number: `t` is the record of age, this object's order is not, and the
// two are allowed to disagree.
const TOOL_VERSIONS = {
  toolMemberOf:     { t: 34, v: "1.0", note: "1.0: in production as build 304 — the version says a customer can open it, and the BETA chip stays while it proves itself against real tenants. 0.2: the THIRD surface is read too — entitlement-management auto-assignment policies, which 0.1 named as a gap and pointed elsewhere for. It is the hardest of the three to look at by hand, because the rule sits in specificAllowedTargets on a policy inside an access package inside a catalog and no view in the portal lists them across the tenant. Read through the same candidate ladder js/groupuse.js uses, since the relationship name moved between beta and v1.0 and ENCA runs on beta; the line is drawn where Microsoft's own script draws it, keeping only policies that carry automaticRequestSettings, because a request-based policy has no rule to freeze. Severity comes from requestAccessForAllowedTargets: a policy handing out an access package TODAY is critical, one defined but not assigning is high, and removeAccessWhenTargetLeavesAllowedTargets is called out separately because it is the quiet half — with the rule frozen nobody ever leaves the scope, so revocation stops happening while the setting still reads as on. Needs EntitlementManagement.Read.All, asked once on the run click and degrading to NOT READ when declined, and a new allSurfaces fact drives a genuinely different clean verdict: all three read means nothing to migrate, two of three means not a clean tenant yet, said in those words on screen and in the report. 0.1: TEMPORARY tool — the memberOf dynamic rule operator has been in public preview since 2022 and Microsoft ends that preview on 3 November 2026. Rules using it do NOT fail on that date: they stop updating and stay in their last known state, so a frozen group keeps handing out whatever membership it held that day, joiners are never covered and leavers are never removed, and nothing anywhere on screen says so. Reads the tenant's dynamic groups (filtered server-side to DynamicMembership, so a directory with forty thousand static groups costs nothing) and its dynamic administrative units, detects the operator by its documented user.memberof / device.memberof shape rather than the bare word, and reports anything that says memberof WITHOUT matching that shape as read-by-hand instead of dropping it — a scanner that quietly discards what it is unsure about is a scanner whose clean report means nothing. Then it goes further than a scan: every affected group is crossed against the Conditional Access policies ENCA already holds, which turns the retirement into a sentence with consequences in it — an EXCLUSION that stops shrinking is a permanent bypass, an INCLUSION that stops growing is an enforcement gap, and both are named per policy with the policy's own state. Group-based licensing, Entra's own membershipRuleProcessingState (a rule already Paused is already frozen), and the source groups each rule points at are read too, so a rule naming a source group the directory no longer has is flagged as stale TODAY, months before the deadline. Verdicts are critical (an enforced policy or a licence assignment rides on it), high (a real consumer, none of it enforcing), review (no consumer ENCA can see) and read-by-hand. Restricted-management administrative units are called out separately, because their membership decides who is shielded from tenant-wide admins. Owners of the affected groups are read for the owner notification email, which asks each owner to replace the rule, convert to assigned, or say it can go; administrative units have no owner in Entra and the modal says so rather than dropping them. Countdown, Markdown report and full CSV export. READS ONLY and asks for no extra consent — rewriting a membership rule changes who a Conditional Access policy hits, and that decision belongs to a person in the portal. Shipped reading two of the three surfaces Microsoft names and saying so outright, so a clean report was never mistaken for a clean tenant; the third arrived in 0.2. Lives in the Temporary tools section at the top of the home page and is removed once the retirement has passed" },
  toolSmsVoice:     { t: 33, v: "1.4", note: "1.4: the panel says WHO changed passkey dynamic migration, and when. A 🕓 Who changed this? button reads the directory audit log for edits to the authentication methods policy and puts the answer under the state strip - the transition in the strip's own words (not paused → paused), the person or app that made it, their IP and the time - with every policy edit in the window behind a Show all. It is its own button and its own scope, asked for on the click: a tenant that refuses AuditLog.Read.All still sees the value, it just does not see the name, and the strip above is left exactly as it was. Three answers, not one: the property changed, or the policy was edited without Entra diffing the nested property - said as that, because putting a name against a change somebody may not have made is worse than saying it is unclear - or nothing in the window at all, which is reported as the window and not as history, since retention is licence-bound at roughly 30 days. This is a read; nothing about it can change the setting. 1.3: the dynamic-migration panel is its own block above the tenant scan instead of the first card inside it — starting a scan used to wipe it off the screen, value and all — and the answer is now a coloured state strip (PAUSED / NOT PAUSED / NOT CHECKED YET / COULD NOT READ) with the raw property value and the time it was read under it, a spinner while reading, a flash on every update and a toast, so a re-read that finds the same value still visibly answers. 1.2: passkey dynamic migration — the Graph-only opt-out from Microsoft's own 1 September 2026 rollout, which has no control anywhere in the Entra admin center — is read and set from the tool: 🔎 Check reads optOutSettings.passkeyDynamicMigration, and one action button pauses it (true) or resumes it (false), behind an acknowledgement that names what it does NOT do — the 1 February 2027 retirement does not move. The write is verified by reading the property back, because a tenant that does not expose it can accept the PATCH and change nothing. This is the tool's only write; needs Policy.ReadWrite.AuthenticationMethod and Authentication Policy Administrator, both asked for on the click. Background: Roy Klooster (rksolutions.nl). 1.1: a Phone role column states outright what the verdict only implied — whether the phone is the user's ONLY MFA method, the DEFAULT their prompts actually use today (read from the preferred-method field, both its dialects), or a backup next to something better; in the summary, the table, the Markdown report and as two explicit yes/no columns in the CSV. The default matters even when a passkey is registered: those users FEEL the retirement first. 1.0: in production as build 299 — the only-here chip comes off on both channels and the version says a customer can open it; the BETA chip stays while it proves itself. 0.2: the Enabled column is filled in on an ALL USERS scope — the registration report the user list comes from carries no accountEnabled, so every row showed a question mark; one paged id-plus-accountEnabled read over the directory fills the flag, and only a user past the read cap keeps the honest question mark. Direct user targets get the same fix; TEMPORARY tool — Microsoft retires its own SMS and voice MFA delivery on 1 February 2027, and from 1 September 2026 users still enabled for SMS or voice are auto-enabled for passkeys and nudged at sign-in; after the retirement a user whose ONLY MFA method is a phone number gets a BLOCKING passkey-registration prompt with no opt-out. Reads the sms and voice authentication method configurations the way Microsoft's Get-SmsVoicePolicyUsers.ps1 does (state, registration campaign, include and exclude targets resolved to names), then goes further than the script: the scope expanded to the actual users (transitive group members, direct targets, ALL USERS via the registration report or a capped directory read), joined with the registration-details report (AuditLog.Read.All asked once on the run click; a refusal degrades the run to scope-only instead of killing it) so every user carries a verdict — phone as the only MFA method (locked out after 1 Feb 2027), phone plus other MFA (migrate), phishing-resistant already registered (ready), in scope with no phone method registered (clean). Countdown to both dates, the temporary opt-out (passkeyDynamicMigration) surfaced when readable, per-verdict filter chips, capped on-screen table, Markdown report and full CSV export. Lives in its own Temporary tools section at the top of the home page, and is removed once the retirement has passed" },
  toolPolicies:     { t: 1, v: "2.9", note: "2.9: the green action bar slid underneath the toolbar and took its own first line with it \u2014 on a two-row bar that hid N policies in view entirely and left a strip of buttons apparently floating under the filters. The sticky offsets were measured at a few chosen moments, and these boxes change height for reasons nothing calls a resize: chips rendering after the policies load, Select all growing from (0) to (105), a second open tab wrapping the tool nav, the toolbar wrapping its actions. A ResizeObserver on the header, the tool nav and the toolbar recomputes when they actually move; 2.8: \ud83d\udc65 Assign gains an eighth action \u2014 RESTORE each policy's own CAxxx-Exclusion group. The only action that aims a DIFFERENT group at every policy: the CA number in the name resolves to its exclusion group through the baseline catalog (derived by convention, and labelled so, when the catalog has no such number). Step 2 is a drift report \u2014 already correct / lost the reference / group not in this tenant / no convention group \u2014 with only the repairs pre-ticked, missing groups creatable from their templates, and the whole tenant as a scope for the sweep. Additive only; tenant-wide asks for the typed ALL, because an exclusion widens a policy. Also fixes the 2.7 REMOVE panel, which threw \"asAssignedGroups is not defined\" for anybody who had not visited the Directory-roles branch first \u2014 its helpers were declared inside that branch and are at module level now.;2.7: the two REMOVE actions in \ud83d\udc65 Assign groups or roles now list the assignment the selected policies ACTUALLY have \u2014 pre-ticked, so the work is unticking what should stay \u2014 instead of the baseline catalog, where ticking a group the policy never referenced did nothing and the group you wanted to take off might not appear at all. Names are read per id, a group the directory no longer has is labelled a dangling reference rather than shown as its own GUID, and each row says how many of the selected policies carry it. A tenant-wide removal now needs the same typed ALL as a tenant-wide rewrite; the expanded card carries the policy id, and every attribute built from an id is escaped;2.5: a guest/external selection names its user types instead of collapsing to one line — which of the six are in it decides whether the policy reaches your CSP partner's delegated admins, and an omitted service provider is called out as NOT service providers; a selection scoped to named tenants says how many;the per-policy what-if flow closes with a 🎯 CA result card: the actual sign-in verdict (denied / succeeds after which controls / session shaping) plus the tenant reality — Enforced today, report-only (recorded, not enforced) or Off (becomes real when switched On);list and summary say how many more include entries there are instead of showing only the first;usable on a phone — non-sticky toolbar, single-column policy cards, scrolling state filters, scrolling list table, two-row action bar;cards / list / settings matrix, persona grouping, dependency inspector, full-screen matrix, selection actions, delete with typed confirmation, per-policy what-if flow, per-persona apply flow, housekeeping (delete superseded Off versions)" },
  toolDocument:     { t: 2, v: "1.7", note: "1.7: on a baseline tenant the document describes the BASELINE CATALOG, not the tenant — scoped to the persona CAxxx policies through the same helper 🗄 Backup uses, with the scope stated in the modal and carried into the deliverable itself (PDF cover line, Markdown blockquote, Word title, SCOPE.txt beside the PNGs). The restricted-unit appendix is deliberately not filtered: those units are the baseline’s own persona vaults.;1.6 (R28): the restricted-unit pages carry this tenant\u2019s group \u2192 persona mapping, so a group sitting in a vault its name does not explain is accounted for in the document rather than looking like somebody\u2019s mistake;1.5 (R27): Word, PDF, Markdown and the PNG bundle can include auditor-ready restricted-administrative-unit pages in the same document as the policies — per unit: persona, protected groups, every CA include/exclude dependency, scoped administrators and role-definition-verified group-membership capability, with no-admin, empty, frozen and unreadable states called out. The appendix reads its own data and fails soft: a denied or broken optional integration never blocks the policy document. Word, PDF, PNG and PNG-bundle export with tenant branding" },
  toolAnalyze:      { t: 3, v: "1.9", note: "1.9: the tool renders inside the List Policies screen, and that screen's Cards / List / Matrix picker was left showing over it \u2014 switching AWAY from Gap analyse rather than within it, with nothing highlighted, and its Matrix meaning a different grid from this tool's own Matrix tab. Replaced here by one labelled \u2190 Back to policies, which returns you to the view you came from; 1.8: a coverage flow above the results \u2014 in scope \u2192 targeted \u2192 covered by an ENFORCED policy \u2192 required to do MFA \u2192 licensed for what they are targeted by, each bar against the same denominator so the drops are readable at a glance; every drop clicks through to exactly those users, and the drop sets are deliberately not the summary cards\u2019 sets. The licence stage reads its own data (assignedLicenses on the user read it already does, plus /subscribedSkus) through LicGap.licenceOf, so \ud83c\udfab Licence gap and this tool cannot disagree about a user \u2014 and a refused read renders as NOT READ rather than as zero;1.7 (R29): a run can be scoped to named users or groups — a group expanded to its members, nothing read tenant-wide, and the result says what it was scoped to;users × policies impact matrix, group filters, standalone HTML report, scoped matrix columns" },
  toolGapCheck:     { t: 8, v: "1.7", note: "scorecard pillars and signals are clickable and filter the findings list to their related categories (severity chips count the filtered view, ✕ chip or All clears);legacy-auth per-policy finding correlates with the tenant's dedicated block — LOW when an enabled tenant-wide block covers it, MEDIUM when the block is narrower, HIGH when there is none — and the scorecard signal mirrors it (100 full / 70 partial / 50 report-only / 0);Zero Trust scorecard (3 pillars, weighted 0-100 signals) on the summary and in the Markdown export; 7 new checks: risk-based-policy gaps, Microsoft-managed policy detection (incl. disabled phantom drafts), platform-scoping without an unknown-platform block, named-location hygiene, MFA client-app-type coverage, disabled resilience defaults, authentication-context / Protected Actions validation; bypass checks, persona × control matrix, deployed-but-Off state, Markdown export" },
  toolExclusions:   { t: 9, v: "1.6", note: "exclusion × policy matrix (default, unmerged), effective-user view and risk review (governance flags per policy, patterns after Tiago S. Carvalho), click-to-filter rows and columns, group member list, CSV and Markdown" },
  toolValidator:    { t: 13, v: "1.6", note: "per-policy sign-in simulation report ported from Jasper Baes' Conditional Access Validator — expected control per user/app/client/location/platform/risk, inverted 'no control' for excluded scope, report-only toggle, filter/search, Markdown export (no Maester code, representative placeholder users), click a policy name to open its card — out of BETA" },
  toolWhatIf:       { t: 14, v: "1.3", note: "1.3 (R30): the policies that did not apply filter by persona, CA number and reason, with a count per reason first — the same CA ranges List Policies groups by, through the same helper;1.2 (R31): the target resource is searched by name over the tenant's own service principals instead of asking for a raw GUID; a pasted id is resolved to a name for confirmation, and an id with no service principal here is reported rather than refused because a policy can legitimately name one;1.1: the verdict names the policy that would BLOCK — clickable, and marked in the list — and no longer counts report-only policies as blocking: a scenario whose only Block policy was staged came back as \u201caccess would be blocked\u201d for a sign-in that succeeds today. Report-only blocks are reported as \u201cwould block once enforced\u201d, and the Markdown export carries the same. Entra Conditional Access What If tool re-implementation — simulate a sign-in (identity, resource, platform, client app, IP/country, device state, risks, auth flow) and get the policies that apply with their grant/session controls plus the policies that don't and the first unmet condition; named-location CIDR and country matching, Markdown export" },
  toolGroupUse:     { t: 19, v: "1.1", note: "1.1: renamed from Group Analyzer to User or Group analyzer — the tool has taken a user as well as a group since 0.1, and the old name made half of what it does invisible from the home page. Name only: tile, sidebar, header, Help and cross-references; report file names and the tool id are unchanged, and Jasper Baes' Microsoft Cloud Group Analyzer keeps its own name in the credit. 1.0: out of BETA — where an Entra group is actually used: 23 services across Entra ID (nesting, administrative units, directory roles incl. PIM eligibility, enterprise apps, group-based licensing, authentication methods policy, Conditional Access, access packages, admin-consent reviewers, registration state), Intune (enrolment restrictions, compliance, configuration profiles, scripts, app protection, app configuration, app assignments, Autopilot, Windows update profiles), Microsoft 365 (M365 group / Team) and Azure RBAC across subscriptions and management groups; one group or user, or a tenant-wide sweep with an unused-groups view; parent-group inheritance shown per hit; per-area consent; Markdown, standalone HTML and CSV export. 0.2: sweep rows open a popup built from the sweep data instead of re-scanning (with Deep analyze for parent expansion), HTML report added, Intune scripts now request DeviceManagementScripts.Read.All and entitlement management uses single-level expands with a candidate ladder (both were failing), and every failure says which permission and which role it needs. 0.3: sweep can be narrowed by name prefix / suffix / substring, and drilling into a group keeps the sweep loaded behind a Back button. 0.4: default sweep scope is the groups Conditional Access actually assigns — taken from the policies already in memory, no directory enumeration — with dangling references (an id a policy names but the directory no longer has) kept and flagged; summary chips ride in a sticky strip so the counts and filters stay reachable on a long result. After Jasper Baes' Microsoft Cloud Group Analyzer" },
  toolCompare:      { t: 18, v: "1.0", note: "two or more users side by side: per-policy assignment (included / excluded and why / not targeted) with differences flagged, group and role membership diff, optional What-If sign-in scenario evaluated per user, differences-only toggle, Markdown export — out of BETA" },
  toolAudit:        { t: 16, v: "1.6", note: "1-, 4- and 24-hour incident windows plus the existing 7/30/90-day review windows; directory audit log of Conditional Access changes — policy, named location, authentication strength/context and terms-of-use edits with a field-level diff of what moved, actor and IP, kind/action filters, Markdown export, JSON snapshot export/compare, summary view — out of BETA" },
  toolImpact:       { t: 26, v: "1.3", note: "1.3: the verdict chips count what the SEARCH left, not the whole run \u2014 they used to keep claiming five would-block policies directly above the words \u201cno report-only policy matches the current filter\u201d, and in Per user they showed policy verdicts including a never-in-scope chip no user can carry. The box also searches the GROUPS a policy targets, suggests the names actually present (policies, their target groups, or UPNs) as you type, and an empty result now says which of the two controls emptied it and carries the undo; 1.2: a would-deny row shows the actual sign-ins behind it, the way \ud83d\udea6 Sign-in failures does \u2014 the controls the policy demanded, then the client, OS, location and device state of the sign-in itself, with the failure reason and error code when the sign-in also failed for an enforced reason. 1.1: a would-deny row says WHY \u2014 what the policy demands and what the sign-in brought (\u201cneeds a compliant device \u2014 device NOT compliant, Azure AD joined\u201d), as evidence rather than diagnosis, with an unregistered device distinguished from a non-compliant one; an interruption names the risk levels behind it (user risk low \u00d72, medium \u00d71) with user and sign-in risk kept separate and \u201chidden\u201d not reported as \u201cnone\u201d; the sign-in window is shared with \ud83d\udea6 Sign-in failures instead of being read twice; and the tool finally has a Help section. out of BETA. 0.3: 1-hour and 4-hour ranges — check a fresh staged policy against the last hour instead of a whole day. 0.2: live progress while the window is read — running sign-in count, page number and elapsed time with a bar toward the record cap, so a large tenant's multi-minute read visibly moves (and survives switching tabs). BETA — the go-live forecast for report-only policies, from the sign-in log: per policy, who would be DENIED, who is interrupted for an extra step and who passes unchanged (reportOnlyFailure / reportOnlyInterrupted / reportOnlySuccess, with out-of-scope as the denominator's remainder); per user, the combined effect of everything in report-only at once, worst case first; verdict chips (would block / prompts only / no change / never in scope / no data — a staged policy with zero traffic is listed too, as the answer that should stop a go-live), distribution bar, per-user drill-down, 1/7/30-day window, Markdown export" },
  toolSignins:      { t: 17, v: "1.9", note: "1.9: interrupt attribution narrowed by what the error code can actually demand — 50074/50076/50072/50079/500121 attach to the applied policies with an MFA or authentication-strength control, 50097 (device authentication) to compliant/joined-device or sign-in-frequency controls (enforcing SIF in a browser means authenticating the device for the session's auth timestamp), 50158 to terms of use; a 50097 next to an MFA policy and a SIF policy now names only the SIF policy. No matching control falls back to every control-bearing applied policy rather than dropping the record. 1.8: enforced mode also gathers the sign-ins Conditional Access INTERRUPTED — abandoned MFA prompt, MFA enrolment, device auth, terms of use. Graph has no 'interrupted' CA status (those records carry 'success' plus an interrupt error code), so a second server-filtered fetch on the interrupt codes (50072/50074/50076/50079/50097/50158/500121) is deduped into the failures and each interrupt is attributed to the applied policies that imposed a control; interrupted badge per sign-in, Blocked/Interrupted filter chips, counts in the header, per-policy table and Markdown report, result column 'interrupted' in the CSV. 1.7: in report-only mode the sign-in window is shared with \ud83c\udf9a Report-only impact \u2014 byte-for-byte the same Graph read, so switching between the two no longer costs a second multi-minute pass over ten thousand records. \u27f3 Rescan always re-reads the tenant, and enforced mode keeps its own server-filtered query. 1-hour and 4-hour ranges for incident triage;search box suggests UPNs — the users and apps in the current result first, then the directory;Sign-ins (per sign-in) is the default view; usable on a phone (full-width search, swipeable policy chips, tighter cards); out of BETA; policy names open the policy card (as in List Policies); sticky policy label while scrolling an expanded policy's sign-ins; sign-in log × Conditional Access verdicts — sign-ins a policy failed (enforced) or would have failed (report-only, client-filtered and capped), grouped per policy with distinct users, affected apps and unmet grant controls; per-policy chips, user/app/IP search, 1/7/30-day window, one-click replay of a logged sign-in in What-If, CSV export (one line per sign-in × failing policy) and Markdown report" },
  toolBaseline:     { t: 10, v: "2.5.1", note: "2.5.1: the card reads its numbers out — how many of the catalog's own policies are here, what a number clash is and whose policy took the number, whose the not-in-baseline rows are — and every count chip carries the meaning as a tooltip; the CloudFellows card says by match with both counts too, and 📌 Keep, when nothing is saved; on a tenant holding both baselines the exact-named policy now wins a shared CA number (CloudFellows' CA000 was passing as Joey's), and an unversioned catalog counts only the exact name as present, a same-number other name is a clash;2.5 (R36.1): the count chips on the summary card are the filter; a tenant with no saved choice gets the baseline it holds most of by name as the ACTIVE one for the session (★ by match, 📌 Keep saves it, an explicit choice is never overridden); the CloudFellows E-Admins policies are expected under every catalog as a shared 🚨 section instead of six not-in-baseline rows; an active catalog with missing policies opens on Missing; 📥 Import baseline on Joey Verlinden starts from the live repository read — policy files, Config/Groups, Config/NamedLocations — with the gap ticked, no zip;2.4 (R36): 🧹 What the other baseline left behind — its groups, persona units and policies with a verdict each; deletes only the unreferenced, empty and Off, units first, behind a typed DELETE;2.3 (R36): the ★ button runs a dry run first — expected groups, vaults, routing changes and conventions, read from the tenant — and only then offers the switch;2.2 (R36): the ★ active baseline — the summary card says which of the two baselines this tenant works against and a button switches it, kept per tenant; looking at a comparison never changes it;2.1: \ud83e\uddf7 Update pre-requirements \u2014 one click for a configuration snapshot, a full backup with dependencies and Markdown documentation, before the baseline is changed; 2.0: one view, the table — in both catalogs. The Cards/Table toggle defaulted to the table for CloudFellows and to cards for the community catalogs, so the same screen answered the same question two different ways depending on which catalog you had clicked, and reading the two baselines side by side meant comparing a table against a wall of cards. A baseline comparison is a row per policy with a status.;1.9 (R35): the bundled catalog is published as the CloudFellows baseline — display name only; the catalog id (limonit) and the CAB-SEC / CAD-SEC group names are unchanged, so stored comparisons and Drift snapshots still match;CloudFellows catalog, table view, number-clash detection, collapsible personas, catalog revision 2026-08-20, refresh, on-screen gap report" },
  toolBaselineJoey: { t: 11, v: "2.0.1", note: "2.0.1: the live read decodes the repository's UTF-16 policy files;2.0 (R36): a first-class baseline — his exclusion groups as templates, his personas as vaults, an exact-match naming contract, and the catalog read LIVE from his repository once per session (latest release and the commit it read) with the bundled snapshot as a stated fallback;1.2: renders as a table, the same as the CloudFellows catalog — it used to open as cards, which is why the two baselines could not be read side by side.;Joey Verlinden catalog 2026.6.1, verified against commit 38469a4" },
  toolMsLearn:      { t: 7, v: "2.3", note: "2.3 (R36): the canonical break-glass group is the active baseline's, the other name stays an accepted alias;five service provider (CSP / GDAP) exclusion checks, judged against the tenant's own cross-tenant access partners — inbound device trust suppresses the device finding, no partner skips all five; findings whose whole remediation is an exclusion are suppressed when the policy already carries it; create builds from the bundled group template; 22 documented checks, 16 buildable fixes, apply-in-tenant with service-principal creation and change report" },
  toolCis:          { t: 21, v: "0.5", note: "catalog r4: disabled policies are evaluated too — a policy meeting every criterion while Off is its own ⏸ Configured tier (own chip, own count, not scored as pass: the benchmark audit requires state enabled, and the control says so), and a 5.2.2.5 strength that carries the phishing-resistant methods plus extras (PR + TAP) gets a 'close — remove the extras or document the deviation' message instead of a flat miss (after reviewing Jhope188's engine, which passes any admin auth-strength policy — deliberately not adopted);catalog r3: CAD- pilot deployment groups satisfy 'users: All' (include-group names resolved via Graph, noted on the control), the token-protection resource criterion names exactly which of the three apps is missing, sign-in-frequency misses show the interval actually found, and an unresolvable or too-broad authentication strength says so;in the tool tab bar; admin-scoped controls (5.2.2.1/.4/.5) accept an admin persona group (Admins / E-Admins token in the policy name + a group include) as the admin scope — with a note on the control, since the benchmark's Graph audit reads includeRoles; catalog r2: every-time sign-in frequency satisfies 5.2.2.13, a single grant control satisfies the OR-operator criterion on 5.2.2.9/.10;tool version shown in the header; the benchmark catalog (control set + assessment criteria, cisdata.js) carries its own revision stamp — 2026-08-10 — shown in the header and the Markdown report, so the CIS content is managed and released on its own track, separate from the app build and the other beta tools;BETA — the tenant's CA policies scored against the CIS Microsoft 365 Foundations Benchmark v7.0.0, section 5.2.2 Conditional Access: all 17 automated recommendations as per-control pass / report-only / fail with the exact Graph audit criteria per control, nearest-miss policies showing exactly which criteria they lack, L1/L2 profile filter, licence-awareness (the three Identity Protection controls drop to 'not licensed' when the tenant has no Entra ID P2), overall + per-level compliance score, Markdown compliance report. Recommendation numbers and titles referenced from the CIS Benchmark (© Center for Internet Security); assessment logic implemented from the benchmark's Graph audit procedures, full text not reproduced" },
  toolJson:         { t: 4, v: "1.3", note: "1.3: on a baseline tenant the backup IS the baseline catalog — scoped to the persona CAxxx policies, with the dependencies read from those policies only, so nothing the tenant uses for itself can ride along; a warning states what is skipped, the zip is named ConditionalAccess-BASELINE-… and carries a BackupScope.json. No override.;policy zip including dependencies and terms-of-use PDFs" },
  toolProtect:      { t: 20, v: "2.1", note: "2.1 (R36): routes groups into the active baseline's persona vaults; the fallback unit is named after that baseline's prefix;2.0: the standalone host of \u2465 Protect gains the same directory search as the tab \u2014 one renderer, so the panel, the refusals and the added-by-hand rows are identical in both places. See toolCaGroups 4.5; 1.9: a refused role-assignable row carries the way out on the row itself — a ⑦ Migrate it button that opens CA groups ⑦ Migrate (the conversion is not duplicated; ⑦ finds the group in its own scan), with a toast from the standalone tool saying to come back and protect it afterwards. A frozen row keeps no button, because its way out starts with leaving the unit, not with migrating. 1.8: the fallback administrative unit picker is styled like every other dropdown instead of a bare native control; 1.7 (R28): a group with no CA number in its name is routed to the vault THIS TENANT mapped it to \u2014 the row says \u201cmapped here\u201d when that is what decided it, and an unmapped group says so instead of being silently skipped;1.6: \u27f3 Re-check protection re-reads each group\u2019s administrative-unit membership and the AU list without leaving the tab or losing the selection; a group protected in the meantime drops out of the selection and its checkbox goes disabled. 1.5: select-all / deselect-all above the group list, counting only the rows that can actually be ticked, and the header no longer describes role-assignable groups as optional \u2014 they cannot be added at all, and it points at \u2466 Migrate. 1.4: role-assignable groups are refused, not merely left unticked \u2014 combining the two protections leaves nobody able to change the members (a role-assignable group admits only GA/PRA; a restricted AU blocks exactly those two, and neither can be assigned at AU scope). The checkbox is disabled, the row explains why, and the write itself filters them out in case a stale selection survives a rescan. 1.3: the scoped-administrator grant uses the same resilient directory-role resolution as Restricted AUs, so it no longer fails with a 400 \"conflicting object\" when the role is already activated but not listed. several scoped administrators, comma-separated, each resolved and granted on its own with per-account outcomes in the report;scan on demand (▶ button) instead of on open, result persists across tab switches until an explicit rescan, a scan in flight survives navigation; standalone view of the exclusion-group protection workflow — restricted management administrative unit (create if missing or reuse), per-group protection status, optional AU-scoped Groups Administrator, explicit acknowledgement, Markdown report; the same workflow remains available as CA groups ⑥ Protect" },
  toolCaGroups:     { t: 12, v: "4.6.1", note: "4.6.1: the ADD A MEMBER boxes get the app's input styling;4.6 (R36): ① Check and ② Create work against the ACTIVE baseline — Joey Verlinden's 38 groups when that is the choice — and the summary hints when the tenant looks deployed against the other one;4.5: \u2465 Protect and \u2466 Migrate reach ANY group in the tenant, not only what the scan found. One shared directory-search panel under both lists (js/app.js cgFindPanel / cgFindRun / cgFindAdd, kind protect or migrate): $search on displayName with a startswith fallback for tenants that refuse it, 25 hits, each tagged with what it is. An added group is checked like a scanned one \u2014 Protect reads its AU membership and routes it by persona, Migrate reads held directory roles, member count and restricted-unit membership \u2014 and is held in cgManual OUTSIDE cgRmau/cgMig so a rescan cannot drop it. The \uff0b Add button refuses what the tab cannot take (not role-assignable for \u2466, non-security or on-premises for \u2465) and the same guard runs at the write. FIXED alongside: an unreferenced group in \u2465 Protect had a disabled checkbox while the row said tick it deliberately \u2014 it is tickable now, still outside the pre-selection and \u2611 Select all. Clicking a group on \u2460 Check now opens its policies: the Included in / Excluded from names are pol-links to the policy card; 4.4: opening the tool now registers its tab no matter who opens it — the crumb moved off the home tile's click handler into openCaGroups itself, so the hand-offs that open this screen directly (⑦ Migrate it from Protect exclusions, the Restricted AUs cannot-list, the import preflight, the migration corner badge) get a Conditional Access groups tab beside the one you came from instead of the old tab staying active over the wrong screen. 4.3: \ud83d\udeab disable nesting is now OPT-IN on every create path and off by default \u2014 the property is not generally available (Microsoft documents the permission but publishes disableNesting on no group schema), so a tenant that lacks it refused the field and every create ended in a red failure line for a setting the panel had promised. The request goes to v1.0, the only version whose docs name it; a refusal is told apart from an ordinary failure, reported, and remembered for the session; and \u2467 Disable nesting no longer falls through to the destructive recreate when the property is unknown, because that route sets it at creation \u2014 the request that was just refused; 4.2 (R28): \u2465 Protect routes through CaMap.codeOf \u2014 this tenant\u2019s stated group \u2192 persona mapping first, the CA number second \u2014 so a tenant\u2019s own exclusion group reaches its persona vault without being renamed;4.1: \u2466 Migrate re-reads the policy references at repoint time and verifies the old group is gone; 4.0: \ud83e\uddf9 Archived groups gains select all / deselect all, ticking only what is safe to delete; 3.9: \u2466 Migrate survives navigating away \u2014 progress lives with the run and a corner badge shows it from any screen; deleting from a list keeps the scroll position; 3.8: the create panels stopped describing groups as role-assignable, which they have not been since 25026, and say what they now do instead; 3.7: the create result says whether nesting was disabled instead of applying it silently, and \u201cassigned\u201d is named as the membership type; every create path sets disableNesting and verifies it — in the create body and by PATCH, with the group never lost when the tenant refuses;3.5: clicking a group in \u2460 Check lists what you can do with THAT group and carries it across \u2014 only what applies is offered, so a missing group cannot have members read, a role-assignable one is offered \u2466 Migrate rather than \u2465 Protect, and a frozen group is told the unit must be left first. 3.4: same datalist fix on the \u2462 Members add bar \u2014 picking a user no longer reopens the suggestions. 3.3: role-assignable groups can no longer be CREATED \u2014 the builder offers assigned or dynamic with an optional restricted-AU placement instead, the \u21bb Recreate-role-assignable action is removed, and the \u2460 Check drift flag is inverted so a plain group is correct and a role-assignable one is what gets flagged (with a button into \u2466 Migrate). 3.2: the \u2462 Members add bar fills the group in when only one is loaded, keeps your last pick when it is still loaded, and clears it when it is not. 3.1: \u2462 Members gains an \u002b Add bar \u2014 directory type-ahead on the user, the loaded groups on the other side \u2014 and \u2466 Migrate keeps its options and Migrate button pinned to the bottom of the panel while you tick through a long list. 3.0: \u2466 Migrate no longer scans just because the tab was opened \u2014 a \u25b6 button starts it, like \u2465 Protect \u2014 and it is laid out in the same .cg-panel style as the rest of the tool instead of borrowing Drift watch\u2019s cards. 2.9: the \u2466 Migrate preview counts the members that will be copied, read the same way the copy reads them (direct members of every type), and says the archived group keeps its own copy \u2014 it is a copy, not a move, which is what makes the rollback real. 2.8: \u2466 Migrate gains select-all / deselect-all with a live count, and the restricted-AU placement is now optional \u2014 leave it off to convert in stages and place the groups later from \u2465 Protect, which the results screen offers as a button. 2.7: \u2466 Migrate (BETA) \u2014 move the baseline off role-assignable groups onto a restricted management administrative unit. Each group is recreated (isAssignableToRole is immutable): rename aside as \u201c(migrated YYYY-MM-DD)\u201d, create a plain group with nesting disabled, copy the members, repoint every policy, then place it in the restricted AU last. Groups holding a directory role, already in a restricted AU, or whose role check failed are skipped with the reason. Markdown report; the archived originals are the rollback. 2.6: the baseline catalog referenced CAB-SEC-U-CA1009-Exclusion (the CA1009 Azure DevOps block) with no matching group template, so the Check tab expected a group it could never offer to create — template added, and the catalog and template sets now reconcile exactly. recreate-as-role-assignable moves ALL member types across (users, service principals, devices — not just users), reports nested groups it cannot move (Entra forbids groups inside role-assignable groups), and the confirm dialog finally says the members come across — it still claimed the new group would be empty, a leftover from before build 219;🧹 Archived groups housekeeping (delete the leftovers a recreate renamed aside, with policy-reference and member guards); recreate now carries the members across; disable nesting hidden on role-assignable groups, which already forbid it;④ Assign targets directory roles as well as groups — include and exclude, with Microsoft’s 14-role privileged set as a quick pick;⑥ Protect takes several scoped administrators (comma-separated);🚫 Disable group nesting (BETA): Entra's beta disableNesting on a persona group so no group can be added as a member — three-state detection (disabled / allowed / not reported, since a plain read does not return it), the in-place PATCH attempted AND verified by reading the value back, rename + recreate + move user members + repoint policies only if Entra refuses and only behind a second typed confirmation, and a hard block when the group already contains nested groups;⑥ Protect: restricted management administrative unit for the CA exclusion groups — per-group protection status, create/reuse the restricted AU (isMemberManagementRestricted, immutable), optional AU-scoped Groups Administrator, explicit acknowledgement, Markdown report; ⑤ Import members: bulk-add pilot users from a CSV (UPN + optional multi-persona column) with persona→group auto-mapping, membership pre-check, review-before-apply and a Markdown change report;baseline group check, creation, members × groups matrix, and policy assignment in one tool, per-group member scan, manual group builder, remove-group action, final confirm, assignment change report, pick-by-persona, TeamsSharedDevices template, per-row create, recreate-role-assignable, convert-to-dynamic (in place or rename + recreate + reassign)" },
  toolAuthCtx:      { t: 22, v: "1.0", note: "out of BETA — manage the Conditional Access authentication contexts (c1-c99): list every defined context with publish state and the CA policies that enforce it, create in a free slot, rename, edit the description, publish / unpublish with one click, delete guarded by Graph's own rules (unpublished + unreferenced only, typed confirmation), free-slot count, Markdown export, demo mode. The id is treated as the immutable contract it is" },
  toolAuthStr:      { t: 23, v: "1.0.1", note: "1.0.1: the AAGUID box gets the app's input styling;Out of BETA as of production 294 \u2014 already 1.0, and the chip was the only thing still calling it unproven; 1.0: in production. BETA — 0.2: Advanced options as in the portal — restrict Passkeys (FIDO2) to specific AAGUIDs (Microsoft Authenticator / Windows Hello presets or any custom AAGUID) and certificate-based authentication to specific issuer SKIs and policy OIDs, read via $expand, written via combinationConfigurations (create carries them inline; edit diffs POST/PATCH/DELETE); restrictions shown on the cards and in the Markdown report. manage the Conditional Access authentication strengths: the three built-in strengths read-only with their combinations, custom strengths created / renamed / re-combined (via Graph's dedicated updateAllowedCombinations action) / deleted when no policy grants them (typed confirmation), each strength classified phishing-resistant / MFA / allows-single-factor by its weakest combination, per-strength policy usage, combination picker grouped by class with the live catalog read from Graph (curated fallback), Markdown export, demo mode" },
  toolTou:          { t: 25, v: "1.0", note: "1.0: in production. PDFs actually load: the list endpoint never returns fileData, so each agreement is now fetched individually with $expand=files — languages and the ⭳ PDF download appear on the cards, and the edit dialog lists the current PDFs (language, default marker, view/download); create fixed: the POST carries only what the create API accepts (name, view-before-accepting, files) with per-device and re-accept applied in a follow-up PATCH, and Save now awaits the file reader so a fast Save cannot race the PDF upload;BETA — manage the terms-of-use agreements Conditional Access can require: every agreement with its behaviour settings (view-before-accepting, per-device acceptance, re-accept schedule, expiration), its PDFs per language with direct download, and the policies requiring it; create with a PDF upload (base64 inline per the Graph contract), edit the behaviour settings, per-agreement acceptance summary on demand (accepted / declined counts + latest, AgreementAcceptance.Read.All requested only then), delete blocked while any policy requires the agreement (dangling-grant protection) and typed-confirmed; Markdown export, demo mode. PDF replacement / extra languages deliberately not yet in scope" },
  toolRmau:         { t: 27, v: "1.8.1", note: "1.8.1: the Group personas name box gets the app's input styling;1.8 (R36): the persona units, ＋ Bulk add, the persona chips and the fallback name follow the active baseline; the R28 group mapping is kept per baseline because the codes differ;1.7 (R28): \ud83c\udff7 Group personas \u2014 a per-tenant mapping from a group to the persona vault it belongs in, so a group with no CA number in its name reaches a vault without being renamed. Exact match on object id or display name only, never inferred; the mapping wins over the CA number and every row says which rule decided; the unmapped are listed rather than dropped. Held in the browser under the tenant id, with JSON export/import;1.6: the baseline checklist stopped printing a Scoped admin column it never read — every pre-existing unit showed an em dash, which alongside a footnote about vaults nobody can open documented a tenant with four scoped Groups Administrators as having none. The column is gone and the report states what it does and does not cover; a grant made while creating a unit is still reported, on the row it belongs to;1.5: the persona group suggestions carry the protection check — what cannot be added is listed with the reason rather than offered as a click that fails, and the chips come from a tenant scan rather than from baseline names;1.4: the per-unit scoped-admin field really does take several people — the type-ahead searches the entry being typed instead of the whole string, and each person is a separate grant with its own outcome;1.3: the bulk scoped-administrator grant is searchable — find people by name, pasted lists still work, both feed one list;1.2: adding a group offers this unit's persona groups first, one click each, with manual entry for any other group — the same shape as granting a scoped administrator;1.1: \ud83d\udc64 Grant scoped administrators across units is findable \u2014 its own panel above the unit cards and a toolbar button, rather than a line of small print at the foot of the baseline checklist; it no longer depends on the baseline panel, so it works on a tenant whose units are not the baseline's. 1.0: in production — persona vaults, bulk add, bulk scoped-admin grants, frozen-group detection, and out of BETA. 0.8: on an AU card the add-member and grant-admin pickers sit ABOVE their lists — a protected AU can hold dozens of groups, and with the list first the input was pushed off the bottom of the card. 0.7: picking a suggestion no longer reopens the dropdown \u2014 selecting from a datalist fires the same input event as typing, so the pick re-ran the query and the browser reopened the list over a field you had already filled. 0.6: the help states the role-assignable/restricted-AU deadlock explicitly. 0.5: a removed member or scoped grant stayed on the card. The code did re-read after the write — but Entra directory writes are not read-your-writes consistent, so the DELETE returns 204 and the very next GET still lists the object, and the re-read faithfully restored the row. Changes are now applied to the card immediately and confirmed against the directory with backoff (3 tries over ~4s); if the directory has not caught up the card keeps showing what you did rather than resurrecting a deleted row, and says so. Adds get the same treatment in the other direction, and both input boxes clear once the entry is on the list. 0.4: granting a scoped administrator failed with a 400 \"conflicting object\" on tenants where the directory role exists but GET /directoryRoles does not return it — the code then tried to activate a role that was already there. Role resolution is now a ladder (alternate-key GET, $filter, activate, unfiltered list matched client-side) and a conflict is treated as evidence the role exists, not as a failure. Granting someone who already holds the role says so instead of relaying Graph's wording. 0.3: 👤 Scoped admins now lands on the grant box and focuses it, with a brief highlight — on an already-expanded card it scrolled to the button that had just been pressed, which looked like a dead button. 0.2: each card carries a 👤 Scoped admins button (with the current count) opening the panel where roles are granted — it was only reachable by clicking the card header, so people looked under ✎ Edit, which is a PATCH of the AU's own name and description; the Edit dialog now points at it. The add-member box suggests the baseline group names before you type (those are what the AU exists to protect) and folds in live directory results for any other group or user; the scoped-administrator box suggests users from Entra as you type. BETA, beta-site only — manage restricted management administrative units: list every AU (restricted first) with members and scoped role grants, per-member CA-reference badges (which policies include/exclude a protected group), add and remove members (group by name, user by UPN), grant and revoke scoped directory roles (Groups/User/Helpdesk/License Administrator via the activated-role + scopedRoleMembers pattern), edit name and description, create (restricted flag set at creation — immutable, and the tool says so) and delete with typed confirmation, Markdown export. The management face of the ⑥ Protect flow's vaults." },
  toolRecycle:      { t: 24, v: "1.0", note: "out of BETA — the Conditional Access recycle bin: recently deleted policies AND named locations (both share the 30-day soft-delete window), each with what it did, its state at deletion, when it went and how many days it has left; expiring-soon filter; one-click restore with the right guard rails — a policy that was On at deletion enforces again the moment it is restored, so that restore demands a typed RESTORE, name collisions with the live set are flagged, and restoring a trusted location warns that All-trusted policies follow it again immediately; the live policy set reloads after a policy restore so every other tool sees it; Markdown export, demo mode" },
  toolDevCheck:     { t: 30, v: "1.1", note: "1.1: the toolbar carried a second \u25b6 Check the coverage next to the big one in the run prompt, and before the first run the two did exactly the same thing. The toolbar button is now \u27f3 Rescan and appears only once there is a result to redo \u2014 the Change audit shape, as in \ud83c\udfab Licence gap. A refused consent or a failed Intune read now offers Try again in the panel, which it has to: with the toolbar button hidden there was otherwise no way to run the tool again without leaving and reopening it; 1.0: in production and out of BETA — the compliant-device reality check (roadmap R11). Per Conditional Access policy and per platform, proves whether require-compliant-device scope is covered by an Intune compliance policy, reads the tenant default for devices with no assigned policy, checks app protection behind require-approved-client-app, names OR alternatives, and exports Markdown. Assignment targets are re-read when list expansion omits them; direct assignment is checked first, then transitive group memberships are matched when names alone cannot prove overlap. Unreadable, unenumerable and over-cap scopes remain honestly not proven. Read-only; requests DeviceManagementConfiguration.Read.All and DeviceManagementApps.Read.All on the click" },
  toolGuide:        { t: 29, v: "0.3", note: "0.3 (R36): the readiness checks read the active baseline's groups, units and policies, and the header says when the step prose was written for the other one;0.2: policy readiness names every missing CA policy instead of showing only persona totals, and uses the Baseline tool's number-clash-safe matcher so unrelated policies cannot fill the count;BETA, beta-site only — the baseline usage guide (roadmap R05): the deployment order as six steps, each with the REASON it sits there rather than just the sequence — the persona model and its CA ranges read from the catalog, groups and their restricted-AU vaults before the policies that make them worth attacking, named locations / authentication strengths / contexts before the policies that reference them by id, Terms of use before import (the one dependency with no create API), policies imported Off on purpose, then Off → report-only → 🎚 forecast → enforced with Global last. 🔎 Read the tenant turns every step into a readiness check — n of m baseline groups, n of m restricted units, trusted-location flag, published contexts, agreements (Agreement.Read.All asked on the click; declined reads as 'not read', never a fake empty), per-persona policy coverage against the 99-policy catalog and the Off / report-only / enforced tally — with the missing items named BEFORE the step is run instead of after. Every step links the tool that does the work; reads only, nothing written; Markdown readiness report; demo mode" },
  toolUserImpact:   { t: 32, v: "1.2", note: "1.2: tied to the baseline it depends on. These rules match on POLICY SHAPE and the bundled catalog (T10 \ud83e\uddec Baseline Policies) decides what shape a requirement is written in \u2014 revision 2026-08-20 moved CA205 and CA301 from a grant to a block and the brief silently stopped covering them. The rules now carry the revision they were last checked against; when the loaded catalog is newer the brief says so on screen AND in both exports, and js/baselineData.js tells whoever bumps the revision to walk the rules first; 1.1: the company-device requirement was only recognised as a GRANT, so every policy expressing it the other way \u2014 a BLOCK on everything that is NOT joined, via a device filter \u2014 was left out of the brief entirely (CA205, CA301, CA309 in the bundled baseline). Entra has no grant control for require Entra joined, only compliantDevice and domainJoinedDevice, which is why the baseline writes it as a block. Both forms now land in one item, the text names whichever consequences apply, and domainJoinedDevice (hybrid join) is recognised as a grant for the first time; 1.0: in production and out of BETA, with \u27f3 Re-read & analyse (queue item 84) shipping alongside; 0.4: \u27f3 Re-read & analyse. The brief is DERIVED from the policy set already in memory rather than read for itself, so it was exactly as current as whenever the tenant was last loaded \u2014 and nothing on the screen said when that was. The header now states it (just now, 5 min ago, 3 d ago) and the button re-reads the tenant and rebuilds the brief, returning to this tool rather than dropping you on the policy list. Always present: this tool has no first run to distinguish it from, so there is nothing to gate it behind; 0.3: opening it adds a tab, like every other tool. Its crumb name and its tab-registry label disagreed by one word, so the match failed silently: no tab was pushed and the home button stayed lit while you were standing on the tool;0.2: the persona baseline (CAxxx-numbered policies) is analyzed FIRST and carries the brief; everything without a persona CA number — a tenant's own or interim policies — is analyzed last, in its own trailing section on screen and in both exports, so a temporary policy can never lead the communication. Same persona rule Backup and the Gap checks use;BETA — the rollout communication written by the tenant itself: what people will notice and what will deliberately no longer be possible, derived from the Conditional Access policies actually deployed. 23 detection rules translate policy configuration into end-user language (MFA, phishing-resistant strengths, legacy-auth and device-code blocks, app protection, app-enforced restrictions, session lifetimes, persistent browser, country allowlists, compliant network, compliant device, unknown platforms, guest allowlists, admin O365 blocks, risk remediation and blocks, insider risk, terms of use, guarded registration, Azure DevOps lockdown), each statement grouped per audience (persona token first, assignment shape as fallback; service accounts, workload identities and break-glass deliberately excluded), stamped live now / staged / at go-live from the policy states, and backed by the named policies with click-through to the policy card. Audience filter, 'no longer possible' short list, Markdown export via the report viewer and a text-based Word (.docx) export built client-side. Reads only what is already in memory — no Graph calls of its own" },
  toolDrift:        { t: 28, v: "1.0", note: "1.0: in production and out of BETA — now snapshots the restricted administrative units too. 0.3: an area that could not be compared says WHICH SIDE failed — a snapshot taken before a read was fixed keeps carrying that failure, and reporting it in red as though it were happening now is misleading; a stale-snapshot area is now marked as such, in muted type, with the fix (take a fresh snapshot) stated, while a genuine failure in the current run stays red. When both sides failed, both reasons are given instead of only the snapshot's. 0.2: cards carry their own padding (the shared .list-card leaves spacing to whatever it wraps, which for every other tool is a table — bare prose sat flush against the border); authentication strengths falls back to a plain read when the tenant rejects $expand with a 400, and if the two snapshots captured different field sets the area says so instead of reporting the missing fields as drift somebody caused. BETA — configuration drift against a snapshot file you keep, so it has no 30-day limit (the Change audit log does). Snapshots policies, named locations, authentication strengths and authentication contexts; a later run reports added / removed / changed with a field-level diff, ranked by severity — a widened exclusion, a policy switched Off or a location turned trusted outrank a rename, because that is how protection disappears quietly. GUIDs resolve to names, volatile fields (modifiedDateTime) are ignored so an untouched policy reads as unchanged, an area that failed to read is reported as not captured rather than as clean, and if the Change audit tool has already run, each drifted object names who changed it. Markdown report. No server, nothing uploaded" },
  toolLicGap:       { t: 31, v: "1.0", note: "1.0: in production and out of BETA. A tool a customer can open is not a 0.x thing \u2014 whatever it accumulated on the beta channel, the BETA chip was what said still proving itself, and that has come off; 0.13: a failed tenant read left no way to retry \u2014 the toolbar Rescan is hidden until there is a result, and the error panel replaced the run prompt, so the tool became unrunnable without reopening it. The error now carries Try again. Introduced with the Rescan pattern in 25164; 0.12: the user read can be FINISHED on a big tenant - the per-pass cap rose to ~50k and a Read-the-remaining-users button continues the read from its saved nextLink (same per-user rules via one shared mapper, bounded per click, re-analyzed in place), because on a 32k tenant the exact gap tile next to a prefix-only named list read as two numbers disagreeing; the partial chip now says first N of M users read instead of just capped. 0.11: one run button - the toolbar duplicate of Count the gap became Rescan and appears only once a result exists to redo (the Change audit pattern); the first run belongs to the big button in the prompt. 0.10: a count on the card is one click from its rows — the breakdown chips are clickable and open the pop-out pre-filtered to that bucket, the pop-out gains a Category column and filter buttons whose counts mirror the card exactly (one shared classifier decides the bucket, so the numbers cannot disagree), and the not-classifiable pill says outright that those identities are not rows in the list. 0.9: role members are resolved by TYPE — a role held through a role-assignable group is expanded to the group’s transitive users (previously the group object counted as one opaque id and its members silently did not), service principals holding a role are ignored because they hold no user licences, and a failed group expansion marks only that role’s count approximate. 0.8: gap users holding NO licences of any kind are labelled (chip, pop-out tag, export) — usually service accounts or sync artifacts that never consume Microsoft services, so the to-license count stops being inflated by accounts nobody would buy for; adopted from office365itpros’ target-set argument after comparing our approach against Tony Redmond’s Get-UnlicensedEntraP1Users.PS1 (whose remaining differences — report-only ignored, scoped-policy exclusions not subtracted, suspended SKUs counted as owned, assignedPlans-based licence check, P1 only — we deliberately do not adopt). 0.7: the tile’s gap and the named list use ONE definition of licensed — per-user licensing now comes from assignedLicenses matched against the same live SKU set the seat totals count (per-user disabledPlans respected), not from assignedPlans, which keeps plans in grace from suspended subscriptions and made the list read smaller than the tile by exactly those users; users whose only P1/P2 is such a grace plan are IN the gap and labelled ‘in grace — seat no longer owned’ (chip, modal tag, export); targeted identities with no member-user record (guests via groups, over-cap) are counted as ‘not classifiable’ instead of silently dropped. 0.6: several admin-accounts groups can be excluded at once — picked one after another from the same auto-fill field, each shown as its own chip with its own ✕, the UNION of their transitive user members excluded (one merge point, so counts and lists cannot disagree), the result and export naming every group. 0.5: an admin-accounts group can be excluded from the scan — Microsoft licenses people, and a second internal account of an already-licensed person needs no second licence; the picker auto-fills with the groups the loaded CA policies reference and searches the directory live from two characters, the group’s transitive user members drop out of every count and named list (intersected with the member-user list where it is complete, so guests in the group cannot undercount the obligation), the exclusion is re-applied without re-reading the tenant, and the result and export state the exclusion with the document-the-mapping caveat. 0.4: the mailbox check WORKS on a normal tenant — a delegated token can only read another mailbox’s settings when the signed-in person holds rights on it, so the direct userPurpose read fails almost everywhere; but the failure itself answers the question: gap users are unlicensed, a regular unlicensed user has NO mailbox, so ‘access denied’ means a mailbox EXISTS without a licence (likely shared/room/equipment, labelled as such with a verify-in-Exchange note) while ‘mailbox not enabled / not found’ means a plain unlicensed account; \ responses now carry the Graph error code so the two can be told apart, the check button counts what it will check against its cap, and live progress runs on the button. 0.3: per-policy Gap column — how many users each policy targets without the licence it needs assigned, so the row that drags unlicensed users in is visible at a glance; the named gap moved into a searchable pop-out (a large tenant makes the list hundreds of rows; the card now shows the decision breakdown — to license / disabled-cleanup / shared-resource — with View all opening the dialog), and 🏷 Check mailbox types reads each gap user’s mailboxSettings.userPurpose on demand (MailboxSettings.Read, asked once, gap users only, capped and batched) to label shared / room / equipment mailbox accounts as never licensed — disable or exclude, not buy; the labels carry into the Markdown export, and an unreadable mailbox stays ‘not readable’ rather than passing as a user. 0.2: the gap is NAMED, not just counted — the member users are read with their assigned service plans, and every targeted user without a P1 (or, for risk policies, P2) licence assigned is listed by UPN on screen and in the Markdown export, disabled accounts flagged as cleanup candidates rather than purchases, with the unassigned-seat count bridging seats-owned and seats-assigned; the P2 half is always shown, saying explicitly when no active risk policy creates an obligation (and which disabled ones would); a capped user read marks the lists partial instead of pretending to be the whole tenant. BETA — Licence gap: targeted users versus P1/P2 entitlements. The licence usage blade counts evaluated users (who triggered a policy last month); the obligation is on targeted users. Resolves the actual targeting of every active policy — All-users minus exclusions, or the union of included users, transitive group members and directory-role holders minus exclusions — and compares the cross-policy union with the P1/P2 seats owned, matched on the AAD_PREMIUM / AAD_PREMIUM_P2 service plans so bundles count and suspended subscriptions do not. Risk-based policies create the P2 obligation; report-only counts, disabled does not (but a disabled risk policy is named as a P2 obligation waiting to happen); unreadable or over-cap scopes are marked approximate rather than presented as exact; guests deliberately not counted. Explains why the gap exists and the ways to close it, with trade-offs. Reads only, no extra consent. After Rudy Mens' Get-EntraLicenseGap.ps1 (lazyadmin.nl)" },
  toolTeamsDev:     { t: 35, v: "0.4", note: "0.4: ROLLBACK. Before the PATCH, a JSON file with the group as it is — rule, processing state, group types, and the members of an assigned group — plus the exact PATCH (and PowerShell) that restores it is downloaded; a ⎌ Roll back button after the write restores it in the same session. The user-suite markers now prefer the SharePoint plans whenever the tenant's suites carry them and derive a plan only for a suite they do not cover — a derived plan that sits in every suite could be one admins disable per user. 0.3: the people holding a device licence on their own account are a TABLE — account, UPN, every licence on it with the device SKUs marked, enabled or disabled — up to 100 rows, on screen and in the Markdown, instead of a run-on line of names. 0.2: the rule gets a NOT half — an account that also holds a user suite (E1/E3/E5, F1/F3, A3/A5, Business) is a person and stays out, whatever device licence sits on it; the suite markers are derived from the tenant's SKUs (smallest set covering every suite, SharePoint plans as the static fallback), the preview applies the same NOT, and the people holding a device licence on their own account are counted and named. 0.1: new. Reads the tenant's subscribed SKUs and keeps the service plans that exist ONLY in Teams device licences (Rooms, Teams Shared Space, Phone resource accounts) — the plans a dynamic rule can use without pulling E5 users in — builds the membership rule from them, previews how many accounts it matches today, grades every group that looks like a Teams group (current, update, matches PEOPLE, assigned), lists the active policies that still reach the devices with a control from Microsoft's not-supported table, and replaces the rule on the group after a confirmation. UPN prefixes cover the accounts no plan can isolate. Teams Phone Standard is a user licence and stays out." },
  toolLocations:    { t: 15, v: "1.7", note: "1.7: the broad-range finding NAMES the prefix to narrow to, per range and per address family — /24 for an office and /16 at the widest for a corporate estate, /48 for an IPv6 site and /64 for a single network — and the explanation states only the family that actually fired, instead of opening an IPv6 finding by explaining what a /8 is.;1.6 (R37): four findings over what the tool already read \u2014 dangling policy references, empty country locations, overly broad IP ranges, and untrusted IP locations where policies rely on All trusted locations; panel above the list, \u26a0 badge per row, in the per-location report and first in the Markdown export. No extra Graph call. The untrusted check is silent where nothing consumes All trusted locations and where a Block policy or the name itself says the location is untrusted on purpose;1.5: a country location is built by typing the COUNTRY, not the two-letter code \u2014 249 ISO 3166-1 entries with the code and name on a chip, paste normalised in place, and anything that is not a real code named rather than dropped (UK and EU are not codes; the United Kingdom is GB). named locations inventory with policy usage; create/edit/delete IP-range and country locations, CIDR + ISO-code validation, trusted-flag impact warning, typed confirm when a referenced location is deleted, card grid + table view, per-location report with full policy usage, per-location and inventory Markdown, JSON config snapshot + compare — out of BETA" },
  toolState:        { t: 5, v: "1.0", note: "On / report-only / Off switching" },
  toolImport:       { t: 6, v: "2.9", note: "2.9 (R36.1): 🔀 Switch baseline — a third assignment mode: policies land with the baseline's own groups as shipped (created here), the members of the other baseline's counterpart groups (break-glass, same-CA-number exclusion, persona include) are COPIED across before the policies land, a superseded same-policy-other-name is switched Off, and the report lists every copy; offered when the file matches a baseline the tenant is not on, pre-selected when its policies visibly use the other baseline's groups. The plan now recognises an unversioned baseline (same CA number + same name = present; a same-number policy whose name contradicts is never replaced in place), and an Agents persona whose include stays as shipped. A live repository read imports directly, no zip;2.8: the \u267b\ufe0f preflight no longer selects disableNesting in the batch that finds a file's groups \u2014 a tenant without the property answered the whole sub-request with a 400, which read as \u201cnone of these groups exist here\u201d rather than as a failure; nesting is read on its own pass, and the harden ticks write to v1.0; 2.7 (R28): a reused group is filed into the vault THIS TENANT says it belongs in \u2014 group routing goes through CaMap.codeOf (tenant mapping first, CA number second) instead of the CA number alone, so a group like Contractors-NoMFA no longer falls through to persona inference;2.6 (R04): the preflight no longer just NAMES what a reused group is missing \u2014 each reusable row carries a tick for what is still open on it (disable nesting, file it into its persona vault), anything impossible says why on the row instead of offering a dead checkbox, nothing is pre-ticked, and the writes run after the policies import so a failed import leaves every existing group as it found it; membership is never touched and every outcome, failures included, reaches the change report; 2.5: a tenant that partly has the groups is spelled out \u2014 what a reused group inherits, and what it does not; the preflight finds the role-assignable groups a file would build on and hands the conversion to \u2466 Migrate (R04); match & replace: an unmappable terms-of-use / custom auth-strength id falls back to the ids of the policy being replaced (they are tenant-valid by definition) instead of failing the upgrade with a bare 400; failed updates now say explicitly that the current policy is untouched;dependencies first, persona remapping, placeholder resolution, change report, on-screen report, claims-challenge step-up, up-front consent, import by persona, dependency scoping to selection, terms-of-use checklist, assignment mode (deployment groups or match & replace: keep the current policy's assignment and switch its superseded version Off), Workload ID licence check (workload-identity policies held back when the tenant lacks the SKU), retry without app references the tenant cannot resolve" },
};
